WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.158 --> 00:00:01.950
When you think of an image,

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.330
you kind of dream it up first.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.698
[CAMERA SHUTTER]

00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:06.813
[AMBIENT MUSIC]

00:00:06.813 --> 00:00:08.480
There is no difference
in how I approach

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.840
making an assignment
like a "Vogue" cover

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.350
or a personal shoot.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:16.515
I fixate on an idea.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.500
Then, I go out and
make my idea happen.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.840
In this class, I'm
going to be discussing

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.840
what I do when I make portraits
of subjects in natural light.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.820
I'll be walking
through how I compose

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.450
my photos and essentially
all the ingredients that

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.460
go into the recipe
of making pictures.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.497
For my early work,
the camera that I used,

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:41.330
was a camera that I
borrowed from my friend.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:44.322
You don't need expensive gear
to make beautiful photography.

00:00:44.322 --> 00:00:45.780
Whatever you can
get your hands on,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.843
I would suggest to
try, even your phone.

00:00:47.843 --> 00:00:49.260
With what you have
in your pocket,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.177
it is very possible to
make something striking

00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:52.020
and to tell a story.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:52.520
Gorgeous.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.978
It's important to be adaptable
to whatever kind of sunlight

00:00:54.978 --> 00:00:59.180
conditions you get and to work
with it and not against it.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.360
That's nice.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.310
With film, it's a
very patient process.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.620
You're not trying to just
like shoot, shoot, shoot.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.700
That's nice there.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.546
Hold that.

00:01:08.546 --> 00:01:10.250
[CAMERA SHUTTER]

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.620
To tell your own stories,
it's important to think

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.880
about your experiences
in your life.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.940
I mainly want to see
protagonists both look like me

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.220
and be experiencing a certain
freedom in public space.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.170
So when I make images of
friends hula hooping or enjoying

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.580
seemingly mundane
pleasures, it's

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.770
about thinking
about freedom that I

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.740
wish for them and for myself.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.070
The most important thing to do
is to continue making pictures

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.495
and to share them online.

00:01:39.495 --> 00:01:41.370
That's been the main
way through which people

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:44.440
have been able to engage with
my work to see what I'm up to.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.290
There are no rules to this.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:46.830
OK.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.265
Suppress those
feelings of self-doubt.

00:01:49.265 --> 00:01:49.890
That's perfect.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.432
The main thing that
you should follow

00:01:51.432 --> 00:01:54.150
is what excites you,
what inspires you,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.240
and your personal sensibilities.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.580
Now, ask yourself what kind
of stories do I want to tell?

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.800
I'm Tyler Mitchell, and
this is my MasterClass.

